  "image_name": "038d0dd2e0af24790043867d5affe795.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a mountain-to-lowland river basin has changed under climate and human influence. Water originates in high mountains with glaciers, permafrost, lakes, and a reservoir, then flows through forests, farmland, towns, and industry to the lower river and a large downstream water body. The diagram highlights a strengthening atmospheric water cycle: both precipitation and evapotranspiration are shown increasing, along with a notable precipitation recycling ratio.\n\nIn contrast, many cryosphere and subsurface water stores appear to be shrinking. Glacier area declines, permafrost thins, groundwater decreases, and soil moisture also trends downward. Open-surface water bodies increase in area, and reservoir capacity rises, suggesting growing human regulation and storage of water. Along the river, discharge in the source reaches is marked as having no strong trend, whereas discharge in the lower reaches decreases substantially. Sediment load also declines.\n\nHuman use is a major theme. Agricultural and urban areas are placed along the river, and human water use is shown increasing. A key annotation states that the share of natural runoff used by humans rose dramatically, from under 20% in the 1950s to over 85% in the 2010s. Overall, the figure conveys intensifying water use and redistribution despite increasing precipitation.",
